# 8. DATA ARCHITECTURE

## 8.1 Authoritative data store

PostgreSQL is Kea Parrot's authoritative persistent data store.

It owns all 27 tables' records (KEA-DBS-002 §4):

- Profiles (`profiles`).
- User preferences (`user_preferences`).
- User consents (`user_consents`).
- User blocks (`user_blocks`).
- User roles (`user_roles`).
- Posts (`posts`).
- Post reactions (`post_reactions`).
- Comments (`comments`).
- Polls (`polls`).
- Poll options (`poll_options`).
- Poll votes (`poll_votes`).
- Games (`games`).
- Game questions (`game_questions`).
- Game options (`game_options`).
- Game sessions (`game_sessions`).
- Game answers (`game_answers`).
- Communities (`communities`).
- Community memberships (`community_members`).
- Locations (`locations`).
- User location preferences (`user_location_preferences`).
- Notifications (`notifications`).
- Notification preferences (`notification_preferences`).
- Reports (`reports`).
- Moderation actions (`moderation_actions`).
- Moderation audit records (`moderation_audit_logs`).
- Administrative audit records (`admin_audit_logs`).
- Approved analytics records (`analytics_events`).

## 8.2 Data ownership

Each domain owns its business operations.

For example:

- Social owns posts and reactions.
- Polls owns votes and poll results.
- Games owns sessions and scores.
- Moderation owns enforcement records.
- Notifications owns notification state.

Cross-domain references must use explicit foreign keys or approved application interfaces.

## 8.3 Data consistency

The following must be enforced:

- Unique constraints.
- Foreign-key constraints.
- Transactional writes.
- Server-side validation.
- Ownership checks.
- Appropriate deletion policies.
- Consistent timestamps.
- Idempotent operations where appropriate.

## 8.4 Geospatial data

PostGIS supports approved location-based discovery.

Use approximate user-selected or permission-based locations.

Public responses must not expose precise coordinates.

The implementation must follow the data minimisation and retention requirements defined in the Database Design & Security Specification (KEA-DBS-002).

