# 9. AUTHENTICATION AND SECURITY ARCHITECTURE

## 9.1 Authentication flow

Supabase Auth is the approved identity provider.

Authentication flow:

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant U as User
    participant B as Browser
    participant A as Kea Parrot Server
    participant S as Supabase Auth
    participant D as PostgreSQL

    U->>B: Submit login credentials
    B->>S: Authentication request
    S-->>B: Auth result and session
    B->>A: Request protected resource
    A->>S: Verify session
    S-->>A: Verified identity
    A->>D: Execute authorised operation
    D-->>A: Result
    A-->>B: Application response
    B-->>U: Updated interface
```

The session transport and cookie strategy must be defined in Product P0 against the pinned Supabase and Next.js versions.

## 9.2 Security boundaries

The browser is untrusted.

Never trust:

- Client-provided role names.
- Client-provided ownership identifiers.
- Client-provided scores.
- Client-provided vote counts.
- Client-provided moderation privileges.
- Client-side validation alone.

All sensitive decisions must be enforced by trusted server operations and database policies.

## 9.3 Privileged credentials

Supabase service-role credentials must remain server-side.

They must never be:

- Embedded in browser bundles.
- Returned through API responses.
- Logged.
- Committed to Git.
- Included in public environment variables.

Use least-privilege database access wherever possible.

## 9.4 Authorisation

The system must support:

- Guest.
- Registered user.
- Moderator.
- Administrator.
- Super administrator.

Roles are resolved server-side from the `user_roles` table only (KEA-DBS-002 §4.9.2).
`profiles` has NO role column. A role is never taken from client claims, and no user
may assign privileges to themselves through a profile update or direct API request.

Guest Session Rule (D-03, decided 2026-11-20): a guest is an unauthenticated visitor,
identified by a server-issued guest-session token stored in the browser. A guest is
NOT an account. A guest MAY browse public discovery, view polls/results and game
descriptions, vote in polls, and play the 3 games. A guest may NOT post, comment,
react, join communities, report content, or create any game record associated with an
account (guest game sessions are tied to the guest session only, never to an account).
LIMITED TIME: a guest participation window lasts 60 minutes from the guest session's
first vote or game start, and ends on browser close, whichever is first. After expiry
the guest must register / log in to vote or play again. One vote per poll per guest
session; guest votes are counted. Guest game scores are not saved to a profile and do
not appear on leaderboards. After registration, guest-session votes/scores do NOT
transfer to the new account (no merge in the MVP). Rate limits apply per guest
session + IP (thresholds TBD in Product P1 config — do not invent). Guests may NOT
change or remove a vote (D-11).

Every privileged operation must verify the user's actual permissions.

## 9.5 Row Level Security

RLS is a mandatory database security layer.

The detailed table-by-table policies, SQL and test scenarios will be defined in KEA-DBS-002.

Application-layer authorisation and database-layer authorisation must complement one another.

