# 11. BACKGROUND PROCESSING ARCHITECTURE

Background processing is used only for approved asynchronous operations.

Potential responsibilities:

- Poll expiration.
- Notification processing.
- Scheduled maintenance.
- Analytics aggregation.
- Data cleanup.

The implementation may use Supabase scheduled functions, database functions or a dedicated worker depending on the approved deployment and operational requirements (decided in Product P0).

## 11.1 Job requirements

Every background job must define:

- Job identifier.
- Purpose.
- Trigger.
- Input.
- Output.
- Permissions.
- Retry policy.
- Failure behaviour.
- Logging.
- Idempotency requirements.

## 11.2 Reliability

Background jobs must:

- Avoid duplicate processing.
- Handle transient errors.
- Record failed execution.
- Support safe retry.
- Avoid exposing privileged credentials.
- Use bounded execution.
- Be observable.

Do not introduce a dedicated queue system until justified by actual workload requirements.

